@startuml deployment
' What Terraform creates, and which way the arrows point.
'
' The question this diagram exists to answer: where does a credential live, and
' who can read it? Every other detail here is in the README's Mermaid flowchart,
' which shows the *request* path. This shows the *trust* boundaries — because the
' one surprising thing about this deployment is an unauthenticated Function URL,
' and a reader who sees that without seeing why is right to be alarmed.
'
' Rendered by `make diagrams`. Do not hand-edit the SVG.

skinparam monochrome true
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam linetype ortho

cloud "Google Health" as GH
cloud "Strava" as ST
actor "you" as HUMAN

rectangle "AWS account" {
  component "receiver\n<size:10>Function URL, no AWS auth</size>" as RX
  queue "work queue\n<size:10>SQS + DLQ</size>" as Q
  component "worker" as W
  component "warden\n<size:10>daily, GetItem only</size>" as WD
  database "store\n<size:10>DynamoDB, single table</size>" as DB
  component "SSM\n<size:10>SecureString</size>" as SSM
  component "alarms\n<size:10>SNS to email</size>" as SNS
}

GH --> RX : notification\n<size:10>shared secret in Authorization</size>
RX --> Q : enqueue raw body
Q --> W
W --> GH : re-fetch the activity
W --> ST : upload corrected TCX
W <--> DB : tokens (CAS), log, inventory
W ..> Q : delayed upload check
Q ..> SNS : 3 failures
WD --> DB : read token age
WD ..> SNS : grant expiring
SSM --> W : client id / secret\n<size:10>at run time</size>
SSM --> RX : webhook secret
SNS --> HUMAN
HUMAN --> SSM : put-parameter
HUMAN --> DB : scripts/authorize.py --table

note bottom of RX
  **No AWS authentication, deliberately.**
  Google cannot sign with SigV4, so the
  shared secret *is* the authentication,
  compared with hmac.compare_digest.

  Safe because the receiver trusts the
  body for nothing: it authenticates,
  copies to the queue and returns. The
  worker re-fetches everything from the
  API, so a forged notification costs an
  empty query rather than a bad upload.
end note

note right of DB
  Token records carry no TTL — expiring
  one would silently deauthorise the
  pipeline. Inventory records carry none
  either: forgetting an activity exists
  is how a second copy gets uploaded.
  Only the processed log expires, at 90d.
end note

note bottom of HUMAN
  The only manual step in steady state,
  and only on the `testing` profile:
  its grant dies after seven days.
  Google retries a failed delivery for
  up to seven days, so a lapsed grant
  costs latency and not data.
end note

@enduml
